.TH BOSWAS-CALENDAR 1 "November 2026" "boswas-apps 1.0~alpha3" "Boswas OS"
.SH NAME
boswas-calendar \- a calendar kept on this computer, with iCalendar import and export
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B boswas-calendar
.RB [ \-\-new ]
.RB [ \-\-show
.I uid
.RB [ \-\-occurrence
.IR start ]]
.RI [ file.ics " ...]"
.br
.B boswas-calendar
.BR \-\-version " | " \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B boswas-calendar
is Calendar, the calendar application of the Boswas desktop. It keeps
events in calendars on this computer. It has no accounts and never uses
the network.
.SS Views
Month, week, work week, day, and an agenda that lists the next 30 days.
An event that lasts several days, or all day, is drawn as one bar across
its days; events that overlap in a day stand side by side; in the week and
day views a line marks the current time and the working hours are shown
lighter. A month cell that has more events than fit says how many more;
clicking that opens the day. The pane on the left has a small month with
week numbers to pick a day from, and the list of calendars, each of which
can be hidden, renamed, recoloured, exported and deleted.
.SS Events
An event has a title, a calendar, a start and an end (or whole days), a
time zone, a repeat rule, reminders, a place and notes. Times are kept as
local times of the event's time zone and shown in the time zone of the
computer; an event set to
.I Same local time everywhere
keeps its clock time wherever the computer is. A repeating event keeps its
local time when summer time begins or ends.
.PP
An event repeats daily, weekly on chosen weekdays, monthly on a day of the
month or on a weekday of the month (the second Tuesday, the last Friday),
or yearly; every 1 to 999 periods; for ever, until a date, or a number of
times. Rules from imported files that the editor cannot express are kept
and shown as
.IR Custom .
Changing or deleting an occurrence of a series asks whether it is for this
event, this and the following events, or all events. These are stored the
way iCalendar does it: a changed occurrence is an event of its own with a
RECURRENCE\-ID, a deleted one an EXDATE, and "this and following" ends the
series with UNTIL and starts a new one.
.PP
Reminders are shown by
.BR boswas\-reminders (1),
also while Calendar is closed: at the time of the event, 5, 10, 15 or 30
minutes, 1 or 2 hours, 1 or 2 days or a week before, or at a lead time of
your own; an all\-day event is reminded at 09:00 on the day or on a day
before. Events of a hidden calendar do not remind.
.SS Pointer and keys
Double click (or Enter on the chosen day or time) makes an event in a small
popup that asks for a title; Ctrl+N opens the full dialog. Dragging across
days of the month, or across a stretch of time in the week, makes an event
for that span. Dragging an event moves it; in the week and day views
dragging its lower edge changes when it ends. A dragged occurrence of a
series becomes a changed occurrence. Double click or Enter opens an event,
Delete deletes it, Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y undo and redo every change. Tab goes
through the events on show, the arrow keys choose a day or a time, Page Up
and Page Down change the period. Ctrl+F searches the titles, places and
notes of all events. Alt+Left and Alt+Right go to the previous and next
period, Ctrl+T to today; Ctrl+Alt+1 to 5 choose the day, work week, week,
month and agenda views. Ctrl+O imports, Ctrl+S exports, Ctrl+, opens the
settings.
.SS Files of other programs
Opening an
.B .ics
file (from the command line, the file manager, or with Ctrl+O) shows the
events it holds and asks which calendar they go into, an existing one or a
new one named after the file; a calendar of public holidays is imported
this way as a calendar of its own (Calendar has no holiday data built in).
Importing a file again updates the events it brought before. One calendar
or all calendars can be exported to an
.B .ics
file.
.PP
What is read: VEVENT with DTSTART, DTEND or DURATION, dates and date\-times
(local, UTC, with TZID), SUMMARY, LOCATION, DESCRIPTION, RRULE, RDATE,
EXDATE, RECURRENCE\-ID, SEQUENCE, CREATED, LAST\-MODIFIED, and VALARM with a
trigger relative to the start or end (an absolute trigger only for an event
that does not repeat). Other properties of an event are kept and written
back. A TZID is matched to a zone of the system's time\-zone database by its
name, a Windows time\-zone name, or the offsets of its VTIMEZONE; when
nothing fits, the times are converted with the file's own definition and
kept in UTC. Tasks, journal entries and free/busy data are skipped, and the
import dialog says how many. RECURRENCE\-ID with RANGE=THISANDFUTURE is read
as a change of that one occurrence.
.PP
What is written: RFC 5545 with CRLF line ends, lines folded at 75 octets,
escaped text, the UID each event always had, and for every time zone used a
VTIMEZONE computed from the time\-zone database (the transitions of the years
the events cover, and the yearly rule from the year it holds).
.PP
Files are not trusted. A file larger than 16 MB is refused; at most 50 000
events are read; a line longer than 64 kB is cut. Repeat rules are expanded
only for the period on show and within fixed bounds: a series with COUNT of
more than 50 000 occurrences that cannot be computed is treated as endless,
and a rule that has no occurrence for a long stretch (120 years, 400 months,
800 weeks, 4000 days) is taken to have ended.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I file.ics
Show the events of the file and ask which calendar to import them into.
.TP
.B \-\-new
Open the dialog for a new event.
.TP
.BI \-\-show " uid"
Go to the event with this UID and open it. With
.BI \-\-occurrence " start"
the occurrence of a repeating event that starts then, written as the series
has it
.RB ( 2026\-11\-17T09:00:00 ,
or
.B 2026\-11\-17
for an all\-day event). The reminder service uses these.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a summary of the options and exit.
.PP
Only one Calendar runs per session; starting it again brings the window
forward and passes the arguments on.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/com.boswas.Calendar/calendar.json
The calendars and events. It is replaced atomically a moment after each
change. A file that cannot be read is set aside as
.IR calendar.json.damaged .
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/com.boswas.Calendar/settings.json
The settings: first day of the week and time format (by default those of
the system's locale), first view, default reminder and length of a new
event, working hours and days, default calendar.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/com.boswas.Calendar/window.ini
The size of the window.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success (also when a running Calendar was brought forward); 4 when
started as root, which Calendar refuses.
.SH NOTES
Calendar does not print. Undo covers the changes made since Calendar was
opened.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR boswas\-reminders (1),
.BR boswas\-clock (1)
